quantum mechanics was for physics  i mean i as i said i um was trained as a  biochemist biochemistry came as a fusion  between biology and chemistry  i think quantum biology is a fusion  between biology and physics and i think  that's the next big fusion in science  that we're going to see a lot coming out  of this interface between biology and  physics including quantum biology but  also many scanning devices etc  that are used in medicine already use  physics and quantum physics so  it is a very um  fruitful  interface  one of the one of the phenomena that  quantum biology  has is studying is quantum proton  tunneling in dna mutations can you tell  us what that is and  yeah this is  in fact what initially interested me in  um  quantum biology um  dna consists of a double helix we all  know and the genetic code is actually  the  substrate of the genetic code are  protons along the double helix  protons single particles along the  double helix where they are is  written the color of your eyes your  propensity for various diseases  and  is coded at a level of protons and  that's a quantum code  and it was speculated many years ago  that  mutations could be caused by protons  quantum mechanically tunneling from the  right to the wrong place  now that seems rather strange but um  protons normally would not be able to go  through a particular barrier  quantum mechanics the weirdness of  quantum mechanics allows them to go  through a barrier that if they were a  classical particle they wouldn't be able  to penetrate  and that can cause mutations if the  proton ends up in the wrong position in  the double helix then when it's  replicated  the dna strand that's made will be a  mistake we'll have a mistake in it we  call those mistakes mutations  so i became interested in whether this  was a cause of mutation 20 years ago  i went and gave a talk about it in the  physics department at the university of  surrey and they were very skeptical but  jim alkalini was in the audience and we  got together afterwards he was  interested in the idea we got together  afterwards eventually wrote a paper  about it i went on to write a book  quantum evolution and  20 years later the field is beginning to  blossom and now we have a quantum  biology doctoral training center at the  university of surrey with about 20 phd  students currently studying quantum  biology  people will have heard of heisenberg's  uncertainty principle schrodinger's cat  how did that how does that all apply to  the very small  level of life do those principles  shed light in how life functions at the  very small yeah it does i mean  as i mentioned the  photosynthesis seems to depend on on  an aspect of quantum mechanics and this  is a schrodinger's cat aspect of quantum  mechanics genius cat famously can be  alive and dead at the same time  as a  as a um  photon of light is transported from one  place to another in a leaf  it can go through a single path if it's  a classical particle but a quantum  mechanical particle can be in multiple  places at once just as shortly as cat  can be dead and alive at once in the in  the story and this being able to travel  by multiple paths at once  may allow photosynthesis to be more  efficient at capturing energy  and there is a mystery at the heart of  photosynthesis it an optimal conditions  is 100 efficient  no technology we have comes anywhere  close to how efficient that first step  it's not all of photosynthesis but that  first step is under optimal conditions  100 every photon of light is captured  nothing in technology approaches that  and quantum mechanics may account for it  early on many years ago roger penrose  wrote this book the new physics and the  emperor's  mind i if i remember the title correctly  and part of the idea was to try and  apply quantum mechanics to you know the  science of the mind the science of  consciousness you have interests in  consciousness as well does does quantum  biology promise a better understanding  of human consciousness it may do but  there's no evidence whatsoever at the  moment which would  convince me of that  as you say i do have an interesting  consciousness i have published a number  of papers  in the area of consciousness  and  none of them depend on quantum mechanics  when i've been speaking about quantum  biology recently and the biology is  going on at the level of of a few  particles you know protein could be here  or there within a dna molecule say  for quantum mechanics to account for  consciousness protons or electrons or  other particles would have to travel  would have to have this property across  your entire brain  and no physicist i know would um  think that that's at all possible so i  don't think the quantum mechanics or at  least we haven't got any evidence for  quantum mechanics being involved in  consciousness at the moment my own idea  is that it's the brain's electromagnetic  field the brain's electromagnetic field  is consciousness your mind is an  electromagnetic field so it's a physical  thing it's not the matter of the brain  it's the energy generated by neuron  firing an electromagnetic field the same  kind of information the same kind of  physicality that gets a signal from a  radio mass to your mobile phone for  example that's an electromagnetic field  that is in your brain and what i'm  claiming is the consciousness is that in  your brain it's that electromagnetic  field sitting within your brain  structure so would that be a kind of  emergent property as it were from the  other processes going on in the brain  yeah exactly and if you like the rest of  the brain can be considered as a kind of  computer that sends information down  wires called neurons  and but on top of that is the  electromagnetic field generated by all  of that neuron firing  and  this is where i believe  consciousness is  but it's not present in computers  because we don't use that information in  computers when computers are built we  try very hard to prevent what is called  electrical interference which is these  electromagnetic fields  interfering starting to  change the information that is in  processing that's going on  our brain has been coping with that  problem for  millions of years  and we think i think the solution it  found is to actually use that  information we call that our conscious  mind and it's actually this information  that's pulled into a if you're like a  pool of electromagnetism  where all of the information is held in  one single place this pool of  electromagnetism and it's now available  to all of the neurons in the brain  and that is where consciousness resides  let's shift from science to the  philosophy of science as it were you  have a new book  that's called life is simple how o comes  razor set science free and unlocked the  universe can you remind us briefly what  uh ocum's razor principle is yes it's  named after a chap called william of  ockham who was a franciscan fire friar  born around 13 1285  so he lived in the  13th 14th century and he came up with  the principle well he didn't come up  with many other people before him even  the ancient greeks had a preference for  simplicity  but it was a fairly  weak preference william moroccan um  he studied um  theology at oxford at that time theology  was called the queen of sciences  and that was because there was no  difference between science and theology  and scholars studied questions such as  you know what angels are made of what  the gates of heaven are made of  and this was considered the part of  science aquinas a generation or so  earlier  had supposedly proved god  the existence of god in five different  ways mostly taken from aristotle  every thing that moves is moved by  another so there must be a first mover  and  aristotle  also said that everything has a purpose  so there must be a purpose to everything  and he called both god  william welcome came along and said no  this is all nonsense  um  the  first mover argument the final cause  argument he decimated all of those  saying for example that objects don't  need causes they're just there a fire is  hot it doesn't need a cause to be hot  it's just hot  so he used his razor to do that he  eliminated causes that were not  necessary and his razer occam's razor  often uh described in in the way our  entities should not be multiplied beyond  necessity  so if you don't need a final cause to  explain things in the world get rid of  it  and at that time also  um  people were philosophers were what were  called philosophical realists and they  believed that every object like a circle  has a universal a kind of platonic form  of circle hiding behind it  and um  and so a a father has a universal of  fatherness that king has a universal  royalty and the whole world was suffused  with  these universals everything had these  hidden universals  when moroccan said no none of them exist  that was important because  in the medieval world  kings were kings because they held this  universal of kingship which they could  pass on to their children so the whole  structure of the medieval world depended  on this notion of universals that were  these invisible entities  um william bockham said they don't exist  and that stripped away a lot of the  metaphysical baggage of the medieval  world  and then he went on to say that there  are two ways of working out the world  one is science and we do it with reason  we get rid of all the baggage so we use  occam's razor to get rid of all the  stuff you don't need  that's science  on the other hand he was a franciscan  friar there is religion and theology and  there you can believe in angels you can  believe in gods or whatever you like  but don't mix it up with science  all the scientists in the subsequent  centuries took that same approach  they delivered simpler solutions in  their science and they kept religion and  theology out of it and this was really  the turning point of science so my book  i claim it is the fundamental  uh premise of science is simplicity  simplicity is a kind of human value  almost you might say an aesthetic value  right so how do we explain the fact that  it's such a good guide to how the world  outside is there's probably many  explanations one of them it can be  explained in bayesian inference which is  down to thomas bates another cleric of  course but uh yeah he came up with this  uh form of statistics now i'll explain  it with two dice  they're invisible dice at the moment  i've got the real ones in my bag but we  won't bother those one dice is as normal  six sided dice the other is a sixty  sided dice  and then unseen to you i will throw one  of these dice okay and the number that  comes comes up i will say is a five  now which dice have i thrown  people will realize of course it's gonna  be the six sided dice because there's  only six different numbers it can throw  the 60-sided dice there are 60 different  numbers it can throw that's a smaller  probability smaller probability of  throwing the 5.  so  this comes out in bayesian reasoning the  likelihood function the likelihood  function is the probability of the data  given the model so the model six sided  dice probability one and six throwing a  five the model 60 sided dice probability  of one 160 of throne of five  and bayesian reasoning says choose the  simplest because it's the most likely so  for example that was used then in the  same kind of reasoning was used to argue  for by copernicus to argue for  heliocentricity the model he had  the heliocentric model was no  better at making predictions in the  heavens than ptolemy's model  but he argued that it was simpler  so that argument that it has fewer parts  to turn to change so it has a higher  probability of of generating the data if  it didn't fits it of course if it  doesn't fit then you've got to have a  little complex model but if your simple  model fits  you could should go with that all of  science does that  john joe mcfadden thank you very much  thank you  [Music]  for more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today to the institute of art  and ideas  [Music]

